:knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111b2cfd-82b0-5772-b24e-fd1bd23e4b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253819} {:answer/id #uuid "21171d16-53d1-5b90-a331-d3226cde70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253963} {:answer/id #uuid "46c7e24a-c1fd-5962-ac5d-4839e3b8c67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254268} {:answer/id #uuid "6832b881-4d09-5782-a46a-848fa67f6fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254613} {:answer/id #uuid "78be4145-2565-5481-9bb8-5816c8527f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21171d16-53d1-5b90-a331-d3226cde70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253963} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6b1a42-ec84-598a-a9a5-a2d349f20b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9dcfba9-1011-5da0-85ce-637573b456f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122498" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\frac{7}{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\frac{7}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{1!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec50a16-bb6b-5f05-b165-80436402a0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253933} {:answer/id #uuid "2a47123d-f4ec-5210-b67f-61a4869b33fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254046} {:answer/id #uuid "3b29dafa-efbf-5a37-8d32-4a83de611c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254185} {:answer/id #uuid "82e767a8-42f1-58ae-87ff-b6da314b6877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254834} {:answer/id #uuid "c081a7f4-7f7e-5025-bc7b-e08baf4bd4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c081a7f4-7f7e-5025-bc7b-e08baf4bd4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255383} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ca1418-3eca-579f-bc5d-83202ced8211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d62b2335-132d-58f4-9c9e-53b886749092" :question/math-academy-id "q-122550" :question/problem "In a group of six dancers, if every possible pair dances together, what is the total number of dances?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ people, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those people for each dance, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of dances is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d412dbf-4d63-56a8-97f9-9b35e1f750a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,807" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253916} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b6064-5a8e-5103-a78f-a2c0f2e9b9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254054} {:answer/id #uuid "9aef58db-6ad9-5889-81a4-34935e92803f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255035} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf8596c-104e-5f0a-9f7f-a01c66e5d834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255183} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b62d0a-3b46-5af4-9af2-0aff7507291f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf8596c-104e-5f0a-9f7f-a01c66e5d834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255183} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1052dc-4eee-5989-a0a2-a3cf9141dddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4102b5a-9ebe-5326-97ea-80b1e81d8483" :question/math-academy-id "q-122551" :question/problem "Mike wants to work out five days a week. In how many ways can Mike choose five days to work out?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ days in a week, so $n=7.$ We want to select $5$ of those days for Mike to workout, so we have $r=5.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\,5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{2!5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2!\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28decb17-2f60-5f59-a7f6-7cd775afb238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254037} {:answer/id #uuid "383c9e5e-11b5-576b-8182-b0c4670f77ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254156} {:answer/id #uuid "4821aeac-b15c-52cb-80e2-ce26701d85ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254284} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8875a4-0a40-525e-86e4-1aa56ad397a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,728" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254650} {:answer/id #uuid "9e619d41-571d-589f-8e3b-716a7b602217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4821aeac-b15c-52cb-80e2-ce26701d85ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254284} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6f2b63-feb8-5d1c-b0ef-b85df01026cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0df0d84e-0701-5f25-a0a7-891dc8357fe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122560" :question/problem "Claire has a bag of candies with $12$ assorted flavors. In how many ways can Claire pick $3$ flavors of candies?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ flavors, so $n=12.$ We want to select $3$ of those flavors, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,320}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "588836e4-5eb6-541f-8c8b-b6376ce409bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254447} {:answer/id #uuid "a67f757f-f076-5974-98f9-9c295d1adbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255137} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fe875b-a80a-59e4-bcdb-ab5d745e6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255409} {:answer/id #uuid "d1028432-c454-5405-bd1d-355788a24a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255534} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b6eea0-760d-5988-ad2c-b73df215fded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2fe875b-a80a-59e4-bcdb-ab5d745e6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255409} :answer-field/id #uuid "075ced90-6313-50ba-a2ba-09025bda52b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9232c011-690c-5d8a-aa34-6b7d7e62803f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122888" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-10,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+15.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t))}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ccd52-63db-5d28-b78b-2df7caf52ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b97203e9-8d87-5cac-b645-904ef91cae7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,\\frac{3}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387355} {:answer/id #uuid "4f63b47e-4384-5932-bde6-b16ca9aebd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387356} {:answer/id #uuid "5941fefb-b666-5264-9ff1-ea630cf1bd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,\\frac{1}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387357} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8b17a6-4cbf-54f9-99c3-f7a62dd1829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,\\frac{4}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387358} {:answer/id #uuid "53d7fc04-e503-5637-99f3-3e1a31b96eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f63b47e-4384-5932-bde6-b16ca9aebd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387356} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7722d9c-8a8e-51c2-9846-c98ae9635908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12003e8d-c48c-5642-87cc-c7c437d424fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123203" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)⟩,$ calculate $r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}),\\,{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04325cf9-d54a-5e1e-83f1-34996b512ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256814} {:answer/id #uuid "4613da6a-11c4-57ad-b9bc-ce9a8d92fd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257396} {:answer/id #uuid "83d8d0fa-6f25-5545-acfd-ab53e9d8056d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257947} {:answer/id #uuid "a53a4c0c-1058-5236-aa4f-579fc051583d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258222} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a95397-8087-572e-9dd6-fff5d617108b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04325cf9-d54a-5e1e-83f1-34996b512ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256814} :answer-field/id #uuid "f598b175-ab79-5c53-b43d-323efda15bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da384ddd-c430-57ef-b97f-337efd7bbd16" :question/math-academy-id "q-123703" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1$ for $n>1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c474915-3112-5a65-ba46-8702d62ab30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396636} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd4e9ae-e251-5b16-b1c3-5b9bc45cef41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396637} {:answer/id #uuid "4bce095d-cbe6-5499-a685-c442389e61af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396638} {:answer/id #uuid "0b06ae43-fe31-53fd-8b10-6540097b006e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396639} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a0eaea-fc7b-55da-b356-f336150395ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bce095d-cbe6-5499-a685-c442389e61af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396638} :answer-field/id #uuid "520b0925-7644-5da3-a57b-98c58f41393e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9bf7b26-4c35-5b3c-bd82-2290add25e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-123715" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n\\ge 1,$ converge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3809196-0e72-568a-acb4-8f1806b011f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1cec5ea2da4a0b5f141bb24b4addc797defbc48e76cdfdbfab3a303e348b8cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387439} {:answer/id #uuid "d44a46da-960a-5000-b573-28968c62249a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcb2b08bab14ce74347d37a0e896451750db0203ab830b835a83de6b